>
> Answer:
> This plan can last a long time and keep you busy.  You need a character
> that is a pretty advanced spell caster.  Is good to genocide:
> gelatinous cube, mind flayers, liches, rust monster, disenchanter,
> monkey and nymphs.
>
> What you need are: a couple of pets, umber hulk and cockatrice; magic
> whistle; pick-axe or mattock; spellbooks; charm monster or magic harp,
> stone to flesh and polymorph spells.  Your pets can protect you and
> create stones which you can break apart and convert into meatballs,
> which you can then polymorph into better food.  You would also be
> polymorphing tools into magic markers to rewrite your spellbooks.  A
> cockatrice can generate, on average, 20 stones for each monster and
> when converted to meatballs is 100 nutrition, which is better than
> tinning and can possibly be converted into lembas wafers for  16,000
> nutrition.  There is more than enough food for you and your pets.
>
> Eventually the umber hulk will clear out a level and you have to go to
> another level and eventually you will run out of levels or face
> monsters that can kill you, but if you keep praying, you might get
> enough hp, spellbooks and protection to survive.  Besides, you will get
> much loot that will help you.  You will get to -128 AC and that will
> make you pretty invulnerable to many monsters.  To safely pray, drag an
> easy to kill monster statue to an altar, do a stone to flesh spell,
> kill the monster and sacrifice.
>
> Eventually the cockatrice will not be able to stone higher level
> monsters and you run out of levels for the umber hulk to clear out.
> What to do?  Drag all the good loot and food to DL16, eat a tin of
> cockatrice and have a 80% chance of leaving a bones file that your next
> character can use.  The next character can so the same thing, adding to
> the loot and food and after several generations you can build up enough
> loot and food that a character will have enough cushion to live
> forever.  After several generations you will have a posse of prior
> character statues that you could revive, charm, equip and take on any
> number of monsters and demons.
>
> You may want to control your experience level and so be drained by a
> wraith and hopefully when you kill the wraith, your cockatrice will eat
> the corpse and gain a level.  You have one or two moves before the
> wraith corpse disappears, so having a cockatrice near when there is a
> wraith is a good idea and sometimes the cockatrice generates a wraith
> statue, that gets added to the loot.  You will want to greatly improve
> the experience level of you cockatrice and have them constantly be able
> to make statues.  I posse of cockatrice can wipe out a squad of
> Uruk-hai in one or two moves.  Wraiths, umber hulks and gargoyles are
> more than happy to quaff potions of gain level.
>
> I like to have six each of umber hulks and defense pets: cockatrice,
> wraiths, and gargoyles, that way I rarely have to get involved in any
> fighting and can take some pet losses, that give me time to replace my
> pets.  I use a root syllable for the name of each type of pet so in
> reading the melee account, so I can keep track of who is doing what to
> whom and know whom I need to heal right away.  The pets will feed
> themselves from the melees, but is a good idea to check on each one,
> once in a while and toss out tripe rations.  I am trying out wraith
> pets with the thinking that in a melee they will reduce the experience
> level of higher level monsters, so the cockatrice can stone them.  I am
> also trying out gargoyle pets, to take on higher level monsters and
> like, wraiths, you do not have to feed them, but they create corpses
> which are not as desirable as statues.   I find that dragons are greedy
> eaters and not much use and when they go wild, start killing my other
> pets with their breath and hurting more than one at a time.  I
> polymorph a monster until, I get the one I want and then charm them, so
> as to not kill a pet by polymorph.  I have yet to try out demon pets,
> then again, I have not taken my army of pets below dl 10, yet.
>
> The battle plan is to enter a new level with defense pets to clear out
> hostile monsters, bring in umber hulks to clear out walls and open the
> level to a big room full of stones, statues and boulders, build a
> corral for making food and make food, repeat.  I want to keep the umber
> hulks on another level, in the initial attack, because they can get
> hurt in battle and then they use their gaze on some pets and then those
> pets start hurting other pets.  I might take a couple of more powerful
> umber hulks in the initial attack to get some wall clearing done, to
> help in moving around and finding monsters.  Actually, for the umber
> hulks to clear out a level, you need to walk along with them, to get
> them to every square.  You will need to make a boulder corral for your
> pets, while you are converting boulders, stones and statues into food
> and when you need defense, to magic whistle your posse.  To get them
> back to the corral, block yourself in the corral, use the magic whistle
> and teleport out.  The pets get in the way of making food and are a
> general nuisance picking up and dropping things and eating the
> meatballs.  You end up moving boulders to cover things or decide to
> build a corral.  You spend more time putting the pets back than they
> spend fighting, but much less than picking up after them.



